{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 2, :comments ["<b>And the children of Gad came.</b> They were the ones who initiated the counsel, therefore Scripture places them first. Moreover, they were men of greater valor than the children of Reuben, as it is written (Deuteronomy 33:20): ״וטרף זרוע וגו׳״ — and they did not fear the men of the land to settle in those places even though they were far from the rest of the tribes who were gathered in the Holy Land." "<b>By way of the Midrash.</b> 'And a great multitude of cattle' — wealth, when it comes from Heaven, endures; and when it does not come from Heaven, it does not endure. Two wealthy men arose in the world: Korah from Israel and Haman from the nations of the world, and both perished from the world, for their portion was not from Heaven, for they seized it for themselves. And so you find with the children of Gad and the children of Reuben, that they were very wealthy and had a great multitude of cattle, and they cherished their wealth and settled outside the Land, separating themselves from their brethren on account of their acquisitions; therefore they were exiled before all the tribes, as it is said (I Chronicles 5:26): ״ויגלם לראובני ולגדי ולחצי שבט המנשי״. This is what Scripture says (Psalms 75:7): ״כי לא ממוצא וממערב ולא ממדבר הרים״ — not from what a person goes out and labors in commerce, traveling from east to west, does he make wealth; nor from what he roams through the wildernesses does he rise — this is ״ולא ממדבר הרים״; and everywhere else 'mountains' is טוריא, except this one, which is an expression of height/exaltation (רוממות). Rather, what does the Holy One, blessed be He, do? He takes the wealth of this one and gives it to that one, as it is said (ibid.): ״כי אלהים שופט זה ישפיל וזה ירים״. And why are they called נכסים (holdings)? Because they are נכסים (concealed) from this one and revealed to that one. And so Hannah said (I Samuel 2:7): ״ה׳ מוריש ומעשיר משפיל אף מרומם״ — with the wrath (אף) that He brings upon this one, He exalts that one. Therefore it says: 'and a great multitude of cattle.' This is what Scripture says (Ecclesiastes 10:2): ״לב חכם לימינו ולב כסיל לשמאלו״ — these are the children of Reuben and the children of Gad, who made the secondary into the primary and the primary into the secondary, for they cherished their wealth more than the souls. 'As your lives,' there is no blessing in it, as it is said (Proverbs 20:21): ״נחלה מבהלת בראשונה ואחריתה לא תבורך״. Therefore it says (ibid. v. 23): ״אל תיגע להעשר מבינתך חדל״. And who is the wealthy man? The one who rejoices in his portion, as it is said (Psalms 128:2): ״יגיע כפיך כי תאכל אשריך וטוב לך״. End."], :book :numbers, :voice :rabbeinu-bahya, :lang :en, :chapter 32, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T14:37:37.594977296Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}